  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining the Scope**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the scope for \"IGCSE OS Map Maker,\" envisioning a Google Slides-like interface. My primary concern is layout functionality for teachers. The initial target is to map the user interface requirements onto a presentation editor paradigm.\n\n\n**Refining the Layout**\n\nI\'ve decided on a fixed A3 landscape canvas within a collapsible sidebar. The plan involves a thumbnail panel for pages and an upload zone for PDFs or images. Strict scaling is paramount: no resizing, only absolute positioning with native resolution preservation. I\'m focusing on image manipulation features: free rotation and hard cropping, with a \"Send Crop to New Page\" option. I\'m leaning toward React with Tailwind CSS, utilizing HTML5 Canvas or absolute CSS positioning for layout, considering cropping implementation.\n\n\n**Detailing the States**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the application\'s state management, outlining the essential components like `pages`, `activePageId`, and `selectedElementId`. I\'m focusing on map element state, defining properties for image source (`src`), position (`x`, `y`), rotation, dimensions, and the crucial `crop` object. I think the crop should be an absolute rectangle relative to the *unrotated* original image. The user will rotate the image and then apply the crop.\n\n\n**Simplifying the Crop**\n\nI\'ve realized a new approach to cropping is simpler and avoids complex calculations. Instead of trying to clip a rotated image, I can just snapshot the cropped region of the workspace onto a new canvas, creating a new image from the canvas. This simplifies the \"Send Crop to New Page\" feature. The image element will maintain its native resolution and the user adjusts a crop box on the workspace.\n\n\n**Refining the Crop Mechanism**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the cropping tool should function, especially with multiple elements. I\'ve decided to restrict cropping to the *selected* map layer, eliminating the need for complex calculations. The system will render the rotated image to an offscreen canvas. Then, by using `getImageData`, it grabs the data within the crop box, saving it as a new image element with zero rotation for cleanliness and efficiency. I\'m focusing on CSS positioning and standard React components.\n\n\n**Implementing Dragging**\n\nI\'ve decided to stick with basic pointer events for image dragging, maintaining absolute positioning. I\'m moving toward outlining the `App.tsx` structure and associated state. I\'ll utilize libraries like `lucide-react` for icons, Tailwind CSS for styling, and `motion` for animations if needed. I\'ll stick with standard React state management and use `crypto.randomUUID()` for unique IDs. I\'m focusing on the component and state setup: `src/components/`, `src/types.ts`, and `src/utils.ts`, and I\'m modeling the data structure for elements and pages. For positioning, I\'ll use `top` and `left` for consistency with the DOM, with rotation handled via the default `transform-origin` of center center.\n\n\n**Clarifying Positioning**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the positioning paradigm for the A3 canvas, focusing on the relationship between millimeters, inches, and pixels for a consistent user experience. I\'ve concluded that a fixed A3 size of 1587x1122 pixels, based on a 96 DPI, will be used. I\'ll employ a CSS `transform: scale()` on the container for screen viewing. This ensures consistent positioning and precise dimension handling for map elements, which will all be using `left` and `top`, as well as `transform` to handle rotation.\n\n\n**Scaling the Workspace**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on workspace scaling. The DOM elements will live within a fixed A3-sized container. This is scaled down for screen fit using `transform: scale()`. The `Workspace` component will calculate the necessary scaling factor based on the available width/height. I\'m leaning toward a visible overflow with an A3 outline.\n\n\n**Testing Dragging Logic**\n\nI\'m now testing element dragging within the scaled canvas, focusing on reliable mouse movement calculations. The initial approach using `movementX` proved inconsistent, especially with scaling. I\'ve switched to tracking `clientX`, `clientY` on pointer down and calculating deltas on move, considering the zoom scale, which I will test. I will ensure this approach accounts for scaling and high polling events, giving consistent results.\n\n\n**Refining the Workflow**\n\nI\'m now refining the cropping workflow. The crop box will be aligned with the workspace, not the rotated element. I\'ll provide a draggable/resizable semi-transparent rectangle over the canvas for this. On \"Send Crop,\" I\'ll create a new image from the pixels within that rectangle, handling rotated elements effectively.\n\n\n**Implementing the Crop**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final cropping workflow and refining the user interface. I\'m focusing on the logic for the \"Send Crop\" feature. The current thought is to draw the selected element onto a new canvas, translated and rotated appropriately, then use `toDataURL` to capture the cropped region, which is then added as a new image to a fresh page at its original position relative to the workspace (which means that on a new page, `0,0` is fine). I\'m also planning a \"Crop Mode\" toggle with draggable/resizable boxes and element overlay functionality.\n\n\n**Defining the Cropping**\n\nI\'m now implementing a `CropBox` component. When in \"Crop Mode\", the user will draw a box on an overlay. This uses `onPointerDown`, `onPointerMove`, and `onPointerUp` events to define the rectangle. The state will hold start position and dimensions. It will be accompanied by \"Send to New Page\" and \"Cancel\" buttons. I\'ll also specify the file structure, and I\'ll consider using `useState` or a custom hook for state management.\n\n\n**Designing the Toolbar**\n\nI\'m now designing the properties toolbar, inspired by Google Slides\' approach. A floating toolbar near the selected element seems best. This will include rotation control (slider, -180 to 180 degrees), a crop toggle, and a delete button. When the crop tool is active, I envision an axis-aligned bounding box. It will be implemented as a react component and mounted in the main display. I\'m focusing on the position of the toolbar and its behaviour with the \"active layer.\"\n\n\n**Clarifying the Crop Box**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to aligning the crop box to the workspace. No more local coordinates, I was wrong there! The crop box will be draggable/resizable on the A3 page, independent of element rotation. It will be initialized in the workspace, with an initial size, and can be moved and re-sized to the user\'s need. I\'m focusing on user interface consistency.\n\n\n**Organizing the Tasks**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a more structured implementation plan, prioritizing essential components for the MVP. I\'ve updated the project metadata and outlined the creation of core type definitions and a Redux-like state management solution. I\'ll focus on these four components: `Sidebar`, `Workspace`, `Toolbar`, and `CropTool` focusing on the cropping logic. I\'m exploring the reducer state format and defining the initial `ElementData` structure for efficient state management.\n\n\n**Defining the State**\n\nI\'m now deep in Redux-like state design, defining `ElementData`, `PageData`, and `AppState` types, alongside action types for managing elements, pages, and the crop mode. I\'ve realized `blob:` URLs, created via `URL.createObjectURL(file)`, provide a much faster and safer way to manage image `src`s, avoiding potential cross-origin issues during cropping. I\'m focusing on defining Redux actions for implementing the MVP components.\n\n\n**Refining the Blob URL**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `extractCrop` function to create `blob:` URLs for cropped images, ensuring cross-origin safety. I\'m focusing on the `crossOrigin = \'anonymous\'` and how to correctly position the element onto the canvas when cropping. I\'m taking the canvas and applying a crop. This way the canvas only needs to know the correct data it needs and applies it safely.\n\n\n**Validating the Transform**\n\nI\'m now validating the transformation logic for the crop. I\'m focusing on the translation and rotation components, where I found a clean way to match the position, that the DOM sets for the element, and convert that into what the canvas expects. I am translating and rotating within a cropped canvas. Then, I am drawing the image with the same rotation and position. I\'m confident about calculating the correct translation and rotation. I\'m now testing the pixel boundaries.\n\n\n**Structuring the Application**\n\nI\'m now structuring the application, focusing on component separation and file organization. I am implementing concurrent calls to edit files. I\'m prioritizing the basic layout with components like `Sidebar`, `Workspace`, and `Toolbar`, and defining state with `useReducer` and the `ElementNode` file for each image element. I\'m structuring a `cropOverlay` component to handle the crop box, alongside essential types and utilities. I\'ve updated the project metadata.\n\n\n**Defining the State**\n\nI\'m now defining the application\'s core state using React Context and a reducer for efficient data management. I\'ve established `AppState`, `AppAction`, and `ElementData` and `PageData` types, along with an initial state including pages, active page, and crop mode. I\'m focusing on the reducer to update the app\'s internal state. My next step will be supporting fixed zoom based on container size in the `Workspace`.\n\n\n**Refining the Actions**\n\nI\'m now revising my planned actions. While I\'ve defined the initial `AppAction` with types like `ADD_PAGE` and `UPDATE_ELEMENT`, I\'ve realized `EXTRACT_CROP` should be handled directly within the components due to its asynchronous nature involving blob creation. I\'ll test the scaling logic for the workspace, based on the container size.\n\n\n**Refining the Actions**\n\nI\'ve refined the action dispatch sequence for cropping, avoiding asynchronous operations within reducers. My plan is to call `extractCrop` to get a new `blobUrl`, dispatch `ADD_PAGE`, `ADD_ELEMENT`, and `SET_ACTIVE_PAGE`. I\'m also planning to implement the types `ElementData`, `PageData`, `AppState` and `AppAction`.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Zoom**\n\nI\'m now integrating zoom into the element dragging logic. I realized passing the zoom factor via Context is the most efficient. On `pointerDown`, I\'m tracking `startX`, `startY`, `initX`, and `initY`. During `pointerMove`, I calculate deltas with the zoom, updating element position. I will implement a `useWorkspaceContext`. The goal is smooth dragging that\'s responsive to zoom.\n\n\n**Implementing the State**\n\nI\'m now implementing the application state, defining the reducer logic for managing pages, elements, the active page and the crop mode within the application. I\'m focusing on defining initial states, handling additions, deletions and updates.\n\n\n**Implementing the State**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented the application\'s core state management, complete with actions for page and element manipulations, including deletion, selection, and crop mode toggling. The reducer efficiently handles updates to `pages`, `activePageId`, `selectedElementId`, and `cropMode`. I\'ve integrated `useReducer` and `AppContext`, ensuring a well-defined and functional state.\n\n\n**Constructing the Sidebar**\n\nI\'m now building the `Sidebar` component, which will feature page management and image upload functionality. I\'m leveraging `lucide-react` for icons and I\'m planning a `handleFileUpload` function. The function will use `URL.createObjectURL` to generate image `src`s. It\'ll also dispatch `ADD_ELEMENT` with the element centered and the correct image dimensions.\n\n\n**Implementing the Sidebar**\n\nI\'m now integrating the sidebar component, complete with page management and upload features, using `lucide-react` for icons. I\'ve built the `handleFileUpload` function to handle images and `URL.createObjectURL` to manage `src`s. It\'s properly dispatching `ADD_ELEMENT`, centralizing elements on pages.\n\n\n**Integrating the Workspace**\n\nI\'m now integrating the workspace scaling logic based on container size, and it\'s looking promising. The `Workspace` calculates and applies the necessary zoom to fit the A3 canvas. This zoom factor is passed down via context, ensuring a responsive display. I\'m focusing on refining the responsiveness in the component structure.\n\n\n**Refining the Upload**\n\nI\'m now refining the upload workflow within the `Sidebar`, realizing I should constrain file types to `image/*` (png, jpeg, webp) for immediate `img` compatibility and robustness without pdf.js. I\'m focusing on user experience, and this change avoids unexpected PDF behavior by limiting to common image formats. I\'m focusing on the Workspace component, to include the zoom function.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Workspace**\n\nI\'ve integrated the `Workspace` and implemented dynamic scaling that adapts responsively to the screen size. I\'ve finished the zoom logic, and the scale is passed down via context.\n\n\n**Testing the Canvas**\n\nI\'m now testing the canvas integration and element positioning, especially concerning scaling and dragging. I\'m focusing on the `ElementNode`\'s drag implementation and its compatibility with the `zoom` factor. I\'m assessing the impact of scaling on element dimensions and interaction.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Dragging Logic**\n\nI\'m now closely examining the element dragging implementation. I\'ve integrated zoom into the dragging calculations, improving the responsiveness significantly. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the `handlePointerDown` function to prevent drag start during crop mode. I\'m focusing on the `onPointerMove` and `onPointerUp` functions. I\'m testing the dragging logic, including scaling.\n\