  user_input {
    user_response: "The uploaded map image (4762 x 2764 pixels) is rendering at its raw pixel size, making it look massive and completely overflow the A3 canvas workspace. This breaks the core scaling purpose of the app. We need to implement a dynamic Coordinate Mapping System that bridges real-world centimeters to screen display pixels.\n\nPlease refactor the canvas rendering logic using the following mathematical architecture:\n\n1. Establish the Screen Scale Factor:\n   - Calculate a dynamic \'pixels-per-centimeter\' ratio based on the current screen width of the central A3 workspace container. \n   - Ratio formula: [A3_Workspace_Pixel_Width / 42cm]\n\n2. Implement Real-World Metadata Fields:\n   - When an image is uploaded, provide two input fields in the toolbar for the teacher: \"Map Print Width (cm)\" and \"Map Print Height (cm)\". \n   - (Optional calculation shortcut: If the user inputs the original DPI/PPI of the source scan, calculate the real-world cm automatically).\n\n3. Relative Layout Rendering:\n   - Do not render images using raw CSS pixels (`width: 4762px`). Instead, calculate the display width on screen using the established scale factor: [Display Width = Map Print Width (cm) * Screen Scale Factor].\n   - This ensures that if a map is physically 50cm wide in the real world, it will correctly overshoot the 42cm wide landscape A3 page boundaries on screen by exactly 8cm proportionally, retaining its high-resolution crispness without distorting the internal 1:25,000 scale.\n\n4. Coordinate Transformations:\n   - Ensure that the cropping, dragging, and rotation matrices use these relative translated coordinates so that when a user crops an element, the final export accurately references the map\'s true real-world dimensions."
    items {
      text: "The uploaded map image (4762 x 2764 pixels) is rendering at its raw pixel size, making it look massive and completely overflow the A3 canvas workspace. This breaks the core scaling purpose of the app. We need to implement a dynamic Coordinate Mapping System that bridges real-world centimeters to screen display pixels.\n\nPlease refactor the canvas rendering logic using the following mathematical architecture:\n\n1. Establish the Screen Scale Factor:\n   - Calculate a dynamic \'pixels-per-centimeter\' ratio based on the current screen width of the central A3 workspace container. \n   - Ratio formula: [A3_Workspace_Pixel_Width / 42cm]\n\n2. Implement Real-World Metadata Fields:\n   - When an image is uploaded, provide two input fields in the toolbar for the teacher: \"Map Print Width (cm)\" and \"Map Print Height (cm)\". \n   - (Optional calculation shortcut: If the user inputs the original DPI/PPI of the source scan, calculate the real-world cm automatically).\n\n3. Relative Layout Rendering:\n   - Do not render images using raw CSS pixels (`width: 4762px`). Instead, calculate the display width on screen using the established scale factor: [Display Width = Map Print Width (cm) * Screen Scale Factor].\n   - This ensures that if a map is physically 50cm wide in the real world, it will correctly overshoot the 42cm wide landscape A3 page boundaries on screen by exactly 8cm proportionally, retaining its high-resolution crispness without distorting the internal 1:25,000 scale.\n\n4. Coordinate Transformations:\n   - Ensure that the cropping, dragging, and rotation matrices use these relative translated coordinates so that when a user crops an element, the final export accurately references the map\'s true real-world dimensions."
