  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating PDF Generation**\n\nOkay, I\'m focusing on the \"Export as PDF\" functionality now. It looks like `html2pdf.js` or a combination of `html2canvas` and `jsPDF` will be key. Since the workspace pages could get very long and complex, I need to consider performance and how well these libraries handle that. I\'m exploring the nuances of each to figure out the best approach.\n\n\n**Refining PDF Generation**\n\nOkay, now I\'m thinking about rendering each workspace \"slide\" as an image. Then, injecting those images into a jsPDF document is the plan. We need a high-resolution approach since the PDF pages are A3 sized. I will investigate `src/App.tsx` next and wire up the export button. I will install `jspdf` and generate the PDF with A3 dimensions (420x297mm).\n\n\n**Implementing Image Cropping**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on image cropping functionality. The user\'s desire for a Google Apps-like double-click-to-crop experience is driving the design. I\'ve decided to leverage the existing `CropOverlay`, starting the crop box exactly at the selected image\'s bounds. I\'ll modify `ElementNode.tsx` to set crop mode on double-click, enabling the visual cropping interface.\n\n\n**Adjusting Crop Logic**\n\nI\'m currently focused on refining the image cropping behavior. I\'ve realized the `CropOverlay` uses hardcoded minimum dimensions, which isn\'t ideal. I need to make sure the crop box starts at the image\'s full size upon double-click. Then, the user can adjust the crop. I\'m also considering how to apply the crop. I\'m thinking of handling crop application on exit from crop mode, or maybe with a dedicated \"Apply Crop\" button.\n\n\n**Finalizing Crop Application**\n\nI\'m now nailing down how the actual image crop will apply. Thinking through it, a \"mask\" approach is simpler: store crop dimensions (`cropX, cropY, cropW, cropH`) in `ElementData`. The current element\'s URL, width, height, x, y, `nativeWidth`, `nativeHeight` will be replaced by the cropped version. `CropOverlay` will have a primary \"Apply Crop\" action. Double-click will initiate crop mode, as requested. I\'ll utilize `extractCrop` to handle the destructive crop and new image data.\n\n\n**Revising Crop Implementation**\n\nI\'m now revising the approach for applying the image crop. Thinking through it, my approach will be similar to other Google apps; double-click should trigger the crop handles. I plan to initialize the crop box to the full image size within the `CropOverlay`. I\'ll explore adding \"Apply Crop\" functionality, and possibly allowing application with a click away or Enter key. I am focusing on making the crop apply to the element in place.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Export Logic**\n\nI\'m now switching gears to focus on the Export function. I am re-evaluating the approach. Instead of rendering each slide as an image, I am considering drawing elements directly onto a temporary HTML5 Canvas for each page. Then I will get the dataURL and insert it into jsPDF. I\'m thinking of installing the libraries `jspdf` and `html2canvas` using `npx -y npm install jspdf html2canvas`. I\'ll iterate through pages, create an A3 canvas for each, draw elements onto the canvas using their existing coordinates, and then use `canvas.toDataURL(\'image/jpeg\', 0.9\')` to create dataURLs that I\'ll stick into jsPDF.\n\n\n**Refining Export Canvas**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on drawing elements directly onto HTML5 canvas for the PDF export. I realize elements are currently HTML `img` tags, but we already have waiting logic, which could be useful. `ElementData` has crucial info: `src`, `x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, and `rotation`. Considering A3 dimensions, I\'m thinking of aiming for a print-quality resolution of 300 DPI, potentially defining an A3 canvas with 1680x1188 pixels at `PIXELS_PER_CM` 40. I\'ll use `jsPDF` for PDF generation, importing the library.\n\n\n**Calculating Canvas Dimensions**\n\nI\'m now calculating precise canvas dimensions. A3 size at 300 DPI requires 4960 x 3508 pixels. I\'m scaling by DPI, 42cm width, and 29.7cm height, calculating the appropriate pixel dimensions, accounting for cm to pixel conversion. I am working on using those numbers and preparing to draw elements with their scaled coordinates.\n\n\n"
