  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Crop Interface**\n\nI\'m currently focused on enhancing the `CropOverlay` component. The immediate goal is to remove the screen dimming effect for a less intrusive user experience. I\'m also working to redesign the crop tool\'s visual appearance. This includes implementing a blue border with black, chunky crop handles, and a rotate tool modeled after the one found in Google Slides.\n\n\n**Implementing Crop Functionality**\n\nI\'ve made progress on the `CropOverlay`. I\'ve implemented the chunky black borders and handles. I\'m focusing now on the rotate tool functionality; the user\'s preference for the Google Slides-style rotation tool is top of mind. I\'ve also identified a design issue: how to cancel crop without a background click. I\'m brainstorming solutions.\n\n\n\n**Revising Transparency Solution**\n\nI\'ve tested removing the screen dimming. It\'s working as expected, using `bg-transparent`. This approach addresses the user\'s explicit request, eliminating screen dimming during the crop process. I\'m moving forward with the blue border and chunky black handles. I\'m also now designing the rotation handle, explicitly modeling it after the Google Slides interface. I\'ll need to figure out where to place it in the code.\n\n\n**Designing Ruler Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a ruler tool. I\'m prioritizing a toggle button, which I\'ll place in the header next to the \"Export as PDF\" button. The ruler will be 30cm long, with tick marks every centimeter and half-centimeter. I\'ll make it draggable and create a `Ruler.tsx` component, updating `AppState` and the `store.tsx` accordingly. I\'m thinking I\'ll add the black handles to the crop box. I\'ll add a rotate handle at the top of the element.\n\n\n**Implementing Element Rotation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the Google Slides-style rotate handle. I\'m exploring where to place it in the application. I\'m leaning toward attaching it to the `CropOverlay` when cropping is active, for consistency with the prompt\'s instructions. Otherwise, I might place it on the `ElementNode` for broader functionality, regardless of crop mode. I\'ve updated the `types.ts` file, and I\'ll now update the `store.tsx`. I\'ll next focus on updating `Sidebar.tsx` or `App.tsx`, for the Ruler toggle.\n\n\n**Integrating Ruler Toggle**\n\nI\'ve successfully updated `store.tsx` to include the `TOGGLE_RULER` action and the `showRuler` state. I\'ve also updated the `AppState` and `AppAction` types. Next, I plan to integrate the toggle button in `App.tsx`, and I will now make the ruler visible upon activation. I\'m focusing on ensuring seamless integration with the existing interface.\n\n\n**Implementing Ruler Button**\n\nI\'ve integrated the toggle button in `App.tsx` as planned, and the ruler now appears upon activation. I\'ve updated the styles, and it\'s working as expected. I\'m now focused on the functionality of the `Ruler.tsx` component, specifically its draggable behavior and visual representation. I\'m focusing on the draggable behaviour, and the integration of a 30cm ruler with cm and half-cm ticks. I\'ve also implemented the pixel-to-cm functionality, so the zoom will work as expected.\n\n\n**Developing Ruler Movement**\n\nI\'ve integrated the ruler and implemented its draggable behavior, using `onPointerDown`, `onPointerMove`, and `onPointerUp` events. I\'m storing the ruler\'s position in `pos.x` and `pos.y`. I also successfully implemented the cm and half-cm tick marks on the ruler, with a pixel to cm implementation for scaling.\n\n\n**Refining Ruler Placement**\n\nI\'m now considering the ideal placement for the `Ruler` component. I\'m exploring the option of embedding it within `Workspace.tsx`. This approach would enable it to scale in tandem with the zoom level. I\'m assessing whether this configuration aligns best with the intended behavior of an on-screen measuring tool. I will next evaluate the implications of this change on existing code, and continue testing.\n\n\n**Embedding Ruler Within Workspace**\n\nI\'ve determined that embedding the `Ruler` component within `Workspace.tsx` is the correct approach. This ensures the ruler scales proportionally with the zoom level. I\'ve validated that the pixel-to-cm conversion continues to function as expected. I will continue to refine the implementation to ensure seamless interaction.\n\n**Refining Crop Interface**\n\nI\'m now back to refining the crop overlay, removing the dimming effect. I\'ve removed the `bg-black/50` class, and replaced with `bg-transparent`. This change addresses previous concerns, but I\'ll make sure the user still understands how to cancel the crop. I\'m taking notes from Google Slides\' image cropping interface.\n\n\n**Revising Crop Functionality**\n\nI\'m now focusing on streamlining the `CropOverlay`. I\'ve removed the \'Active Crop Area\' label and the \'Apply Crop\' / \'Extract\' buttons. The goal is to emulate Google Slides. The right sidebar will now handle crop options. I\'m considering adding a \'rotate\' tool handle to the image itself when selected. I\'ll need to figure out where in the code to add it, but it sounds similar to where I added the black chunky lines.\n\n\n**Implementing Rotate Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Google Slides-style rotation handle. I\'m focusing on the `handleRotatePointerDown` function. I\'ve calculated the center of the element. I\'m implementing the pointer events for rotation. I\'ll focus on calculating the angle.\n\n\n**Updating Rotation Calculation**\n\nI\'m now refining the rotate functionality, focusing on integrating it directly with `element.rotation`. I\'ve calculated the rotation angle using `Math.atan2` and incorporated it into the `UPDATE_ELEMENT` dispatch, which should update the element\'s rotation as the user drags. I\'ve also refactored the pointer event listeners. The user wants the Google Slides style. I\'ll focus on styling now.\n\n\n**Adjusting Crop Visuals**\n\nI\'m now changing the crop visual design. The background is transparent. I\'ve updated the border to a subtle `1px dashed rgba(0,0,0,0.5)`, to match Google Slides. I\'m checking the `CropOverlay.tsx` and verifying placement of the crop overlay and handles, specifically checking for the `bg-transparent` and `zIndex` properties. I\'m focusing on the handle border as well. I\'m reviewing the existing buttons in `Sidebar`.\n\n\n**Revising Crop Functionality**\n\nI\'m revisiting the crop implementation. I realized I removed `applyCropInPlace` and `extractToNew` from the right sidebar, because the user could not trigger `applyCropInPlace`. I\'ll keep the buttons, but remove the screen dimming. I\'ll test the change with the transparent background. I will also make the crop area cleaner by adding the rotation tool. I\'ll focus on the `CropOverlay` component.\n\n\n"
