    thinking: "**Analyzing Crop and Zoom**\n\nI\'m currently focused on incorporating a zoom tool and ensuring the cropping functionality accurately updates the underlying image properties. My investigation centers on the `extractCrop` function within `src/utils.ts`. I need to figure out how to modify this to work the way I want, which is to crop out the deleted parts. I am working on how to do this now.\n\n\n**Clarifying Extraction Logic**\n\nI\'ve been examining how `extractCrop` actually renders to the canvas. I now understand that it\'s shifting and rotating, but it still doesn\'t \"delete\" the cropped part. I\'m investigating how the cropping transformation can truly remove the cropped pixels, not just render them invisibly. The crucial part now is integrating the image drawing with coordinate transformation, considering `src`, `x`, `y`, `width`, and `height`.\n\n\n**Revisiting Crop Transformation**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the impact of applying a crop when the source image already has a rotation applied. The `UPDATE_ELEMENT` dispatch modifies properties like `x`, `y`, `width`, and `height`, and crucially, updates `src` with the new cropped image URL. I\'m focusing on whether the rotation is handled correctly. Specifically, is the cropped image pre-rotated when `extractCrop` runs?\n\n\n**Confirming Rotation Handling**\n\nI\'ve realized that applying `element.rotation` to a cropped image is redundant, as the crop renders the image within an axis-aligned bounding box. The core problem is that if we have a rotated image, and extract a crop, the extracted crop image will be unrotated. So when the new `ElementNode` renders, it will apply the rotation again, and this is incorrect! I must set `rotation: 0` for new cropped elements. I\'m verifying this in `extractToNew`.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Rotation Bug**\n\nI\'ve isolated a critical issue concerning rotation. While `extractToNew` correctly sets the rotation of a new cropped element to zero, `applyCropInPlace` doesn\'t. This discrepancy leads to a double-rotation effect, as the cropped image retains its original rotation and then receives the rotation again when it renders. It looks like this is the root cause of the bug. I am verifying this now.\n\n\n**Investigating Coordinate Systems**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into coordinate transformations within `extractCrop`. It seems I was mistaken about the origin of `element.x` and `element.y` in relation to rotation; they align with CSS positioning, but the rotation transformation complicates things. I am now reconciling how WORKSPACE coordinates from `cropRectCm` relate to the image\'s rotated position. I am re-examining the formula for calculating pixel coordinates for the crop. I\'m focusing on the interaction of the image rotation with the crop rectangle\'s position.\n\n\n**Refining Coordinate Translation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the rotation applies after the initial translation of the crop box. It seems the core problem might stem from how `cropX_px` and `cropY_px` are calculated and how this affects the `ctx.translate` call before the rotation and drawing. I\'m investigating how the `cropRectCm` coordinates map onto the rotated image to ensure the canvas drawing is correct, given the relative positions. I\'m especially concerned about the initial shift and the subsequent rotation.\n\n\n**Verifying `objectFit` Logic**\n\nI\'m checking if setting `objectFit: \'fill\'` resolves the rendering issues. I\'m focusing on how scaling and fitting work together with the image source. I am revisiting the interactions between `element` dimensions, native image dimensions, and how they relate to the underlying canvas draw calls. I am confident I\'m close to a working solution.\n\n\n**Confirming Update Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the update I had considered earlier. I\'ve integrated a key fix by setting `rotation: 0` during the crop operation and it appears to resolve the double-rotation issues. The update specifically targets the `UPDATE_ELEMENT` and `extractToNew` dispatches, which should prevent unintended rotations. I\'m verifying the impact of this on the various zoom and rotation scenarios.\n\n\n**Examining Native Dimensions**\n\nI\'m now investigating a potential bug related to how `extractCrop` uses `element.nativeWidth` and `element.nativeHeight` within `drawImage`. I\'m concerned about discrepancies that may arise when the actual image dimensions (`img.naturalWidth`) differ from these properties, especially after scaling or modifications. I am now double-checking whether stretching occurs because `drawImage` draws to the specified size.\n\n\n**Validating Pixel Mapping**\n\nI\'m now back-tracking and testing the `extractCrop` function. I see potential issues with the underlying canvas dimensions and pixel mapping after a crop. The `canvas` dimensions, especially in the context of the initial `x`, `y`, `width` and `height` of the `element` and the crop rectangle, needs to be thoroughly checked. I\'m focusing on the translation, scaling and then the eventual `drawImage` call.\n\n\n**Confirming Coordinate Mapping**\n\nI\'m now back at the pixel level, and it all comes down to verifying the coordinate systems. I was initially focused on how the draw call works. However, the `drawImage` now makes sense. The canvas origin, at `(0, 0)`, maps exactly to the image\'s coordinate `(200, 200)` and its total dimensions are correctly calculated by the zoom and other factors. I am validating that the cropping functionality accurately reflects this relationship and that the image updates visually after cropping, but have found an issue with the state updating.\n\n\n**Verifying Box Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now certain `cropBoxCm` updates correctly in `CropOverlay.tsx`. The calculations for the new box dimensions and positions seem accurate based on mouse movement. I\'ve re-examined the code within `CropOverlay.tsx` and am confident in its logic. I\'ve tracked the values and they look right! I\'m moving on to consider how this is used within the overall system.\n\n\n**Implementing Zoom Feature**\n\nI am now adding the zoom UI element, along with related logic. I\'m focusing on its integration with the existing cropping and rotation operations. The blob generation will work as expected with the updated zoom. I\'m confident in the overall workflow. I believe that there are no CORS-related issues.\n\n\n**Validating Pixel Adjustment**\n\nI am now verifying the assumptions around pixel adjustments after considering the zoom. I\'ve re-examined the coordinate transformations, focusing on how `PIXELS_PER_CM` and `scaleNativePerCm` integrate with the crop calculations and zoom level. My concern is whether zoom impacts the precision of cropped pixel selection and image drawing with regards to the bounding box. It looks right to me. I need to make sure I am on the right track!\n\n\n**Confirming Translation Correctness**\n\nI\'m now satisfied with the `extractCrop` coordinate system calculations. The canvas origin correctly maps to the image. `drawImage` draws correctly after the zoom and rotation. I\'ve re-examined the code, focusing on how zooming integrates with the existing crop and image draw calls. The translation and scaling operations are now correct, ensuring that pixel selections and image drawing are accurate. I\'m verifying the edge cases now.\n\n\n**Refining Blob Generation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the image is actually cropped and how a new, smaller image file is generated with the correct dimensions after applying a crop. I am concerned with the implications of `objectFit: \'fill\'` for the new cropped images. I\'m verifying the actual removal of the cropped pixels. I am trying to determine if `ctx.drawImage` causes any unforeseen issues.\n\n\n**Implementing Crop Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the updated `rotation: 0` setting within the zoom workflow. Specifically, I\'m ensuring cropped images maintain the expected axis-aligned bounding box behavior. I\'ve re-examined the component that contains the Zoom control. I\'m verifying the interaction with the crop and zoom states to maintain visual consistency.\n\n\n**Integrating Zoom and Panning**\n\nI\'m now integrating zoom with a panning feature. Initially, zoom was handled via an existing `zoomLevel` state, but now I\'m making it compatible with panning. I\'ve realized `overflow-auto` within a flexible container allows both features. I\'m adapting the container for this and verifying interaction between zoom, crop, and pan functions.\n\n\n"
