    thinking: "**Implementing Copy/Paste**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing copy and paste functionality for elements within the workspace, specifically using `ctrl+c` and `ctrl+v`. I\'ve also identified a key issue related to cropping after rotation, where the crop handles aren\'t updating properly to the new orientation. The handles for rotation will show the former orientation if the image is rotated.\n\n\n**Analyzing Cursor Behavior**\n\nI\'ve realized the crop handles\' rotation problem stems from the mismatch between rotated elements and fixed CSS cursors. The cursor\'s visual direction doesn\'t reflect the rotated element\'s orientation. My `handleCropDrag` function correctly accounts for element rotation; the mouse movement is already mapped to the rotated coordinate system. Now, I must find a solution to update the cursor\'s appearance to reflect the element\'s actual direction.\n\n\n**Refining Cursor Implementation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the precise cursor implementation for the crop handles, considering that rotation is arbitrary. I see that I can calculate the apparent angle of each handle and set the appropriate cursor accordingly. Alternatively, I could just rely on standard generic arrows. However, I am leaning toward dynamically adjusting the cursor string because CSS provides limited resize cursor options. The key is in computing the cursor string by shifting the direction, based on the `element.rotation`. I\'m going to start by looking at Google Slides\' implementation to guide my thought process.\n\n\n**Addressing Selection Outline**\n\nI\'ve realized the dragging issue lies in the selection outline. As I move an element, the selection outline continues to show the original, uncropped dimensions. The `element.width` and `element.height` in `ElementNode` reflect these original values. The solution would be to update the outline or div size to reflect the *cropped* area, but remember that the ElementNode does rotate.\n\n\n**Revising Outline Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the selection outline\'s behavior during cropping. My goal is to mimic Google Slides, where the blue outline hugs the *visible* portion of the image. My idea is to move the outline to an inner `div` that matches the crop box\'s size and position, instead of the original element dimensions. I will start by examining `ElementNode.tsx` to achieve this.\n\n**Initiating Copy/Paste**\n\nI\'m turning my attention to copy and paste functionality. The challenge lies in efficiently copying an `selectedElementId` from state upon `Ctrl+C` and pasting the item using `Ctrl+V`. I\'ll implement global `keydown` listeners within `App.tsx` or `Workspace.tsx` to capture these shortcuts. I\'ll need to make sure to exclude inputs in this process.\n\n\n**Implementing Copy/Paste Shortcuts**\n\nI\'ve made progress implementing the keyboard shortcuts for copy and paste using `keydown` listeners. I\'ve integrated checks to ignore input elements and dispatched actions for copy and paste. I am adding a `clipboardElement` to the state, along with actions for `COPY_ELEMENT` and `PASTE_ELEMENT`. The element\'s unique id will be changed on paste. Also, I have updated `src/types.ts` and `src/store.tsx` to handle the new `clipboardElement` within `AppState`. Finally, I\'ve created an initial state and the initial reducer to handle this new addition.\n\n\n**Refining Copy/Paste Logic**\n\nI\'m currently working on refining the copy and paste actions within the reducer. I\'ve successfully implemented `COPY_ELEMENT`, storing the selected element in the `clipboardElement` of the state. `PASTE_ELEMENT` correctly generates a new, unique ID for the pasted element, offsets its position, and adds it to the active page\'s elements. I am also properly maintaining state immutability. Next, I need to focus on handling edge cases, like what to do if there is nothing to copy.\n\n\n**Calculating Rotated Cursors**\n\nI\'m now determining how to calculate crop handle cursors during rotation. I plan to use an array of directions and element rotation to find the correct cursor. The `getCursor` function is an option, and the goal is to map screen-based CSS resize cursors to the rotated element\'s orientation.\n\n\n**Mapping Cursor Directions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating the appropriate crop handle cursors during rotation, especially how to apply these directions in the Tailwind CSS scheme. I\'m going to create a mapping array to translate angles and determine bidirectional cursors, as I need to convert degrees to Tailwind classes. Specifically, I\'m thinking of mapping 0 and 180 to `ns-resize`, 45 and 225 to `nesw-resize`, 90 and 270 to `ew-resize`, and finally 135 and 315 to `nwse-resize`. The goal is to accurately translate element rotation into visual cursor feedback.\n\n\n**Adjusting Cropping Outlines**\n\nI am now addressing the outline display. I am moving the outline to an inner `div`, to show the *cropped* area rather than the original, uncropped dimensions. I\'m exploring an inner `div` that accurately reflects the crop box size and position. The current focus is to move the selection outline and I will begin by examining the existing structure in `ElementNode.tsx`.\n\n\n**Adapting Rotation Origin**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the rotation origin in relation to cropping. Currently, the rotation is centered on the original, uncropped image, even after cropping. I\'m exploring how to shift the rotation center to the cropped image\'s center, like in Google Slides. This involves adjusting the `transformOrigin` and compensating for the visual shift. I\'ll need to update the outer `div`\'s `x, y` position to maintain drag behavior.\n\n\n**Refining Drag Behavior**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring the selection outline accurately reflects cropped boundaries during drag operations. Previously, it persisted the original dimensions. Now, I\'m working to make the outline update dynamically to show the cropped image\'s dimensions. I\'m also addressing the incorrect position of the rotation handle. I\'m changing the selection outline to an inner `div` that matches the crop box. This will be the next task to refine!\n\n\n**Evaluating Rotation Consistency**\n\nI\'m presently evaluating the implications of rotating around the original image\'s center, even after cropping. While I initially considered shifting the rotational origin to the cropped area, the current implementation, mirroring Google Slides\' approach, offers a degree of predictability. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent behavior, given the scope of the current task.\n\n\n**Mapping Crop Directions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on accurate crop handle cursor mappings, shifting focus to refining how the inner `div` selection box responds during drag and rotation. I am working to define an accurate `getCursorClass` function. I\'ve been experimenting with several angles to translate into specific CSS classes, as well as considering how to deal with the `CropOverlay`. I am now making sure that the cropped image is displayed and the crop box follows.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Cursor Logic**\n\nI\'ve just successfully implemented the cursor logic using an array of cursor classes and angle calculations! It\'s now dynamically adjusting CSS cursors based on element rotation, using the mapping logic to handle bidirectional resize cursors. I\'m leveraging the `getCursorClass` function. It accurately translates element rotation into visual cursor feedback and the handlers.\n\n\n**Refining Cropping Display**\n\nI\'m now diving into the finer points of the cropped image and selection display. I\'m focusing on ensuring the crop handles, the image inside, and the selection outline are all aligned and accurately represent the cropped area. My plan is to ensure the crop handles are still clickable, even if rotated. I\'m going to carefully review the changes to `ElementNode.tsx` to optimize the interaction.\n\n\n**Optimizing Crop Display**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on optimizing the display of the cropped area. My recent focus is on synchronizing the crop handles, the image rendering, and the selection outline so that they consistently reflect the trimmed content. I\'m focusing on clickability, rotation, and alignment within `ElementNode.tsx`, aiming for an intuitive cropping experience.\n\n\n**Calculating Cursor Angle**\n\nI\'ve just implemented and tested the `getCursorClass` function to dynamically set cursors, using the formula `(baseAngle + element.rotation) / 45) * 45`. It properly normalizes the angle and correctly maps directions to CSS cursor classes. Now I am able to accurately translate element rotation into visual cursor feedback and the handlers, and they render as expected.\n\n\n**Refining Handle Cursor**\n\nI\'m now refining the crop handle cursors, after getting the dynamic cursor classes working. I am making sure the correct cursors are applied to the handle, which now uses the dynamic logic. The handles\' cursor assignments are going to be handled outside of `ElementNode.tsx`, because of the updated cursor calculation and state.\n\n\n**Implementing Handle Positioning**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the accurate positioning and cursor logic of the crop handles. I\'m dynamically updating the CSS cursors based on element rotation. I am working on using the mapping logic to handle bidirectional resize cursors, and the current work is in the handle\'s styling and cursor assignments.\n\n\n**Positioning Crop Handles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the positioning logic for the crop handles, and dynamically setting the cursors using the new `getCursorClass` function. I\'m making sure that the bidirectional cursors for resizing are correct, and all aspects of each handle are updated to reflect the new state. I\'m going to start by looking at `CropHandle.tsx` and updating the CSS cursors there.\n\n\n**Refining Handle Cursors**\n\nI\'m making final tweaks to the crop handle cursors. After implementing the dynamic cursor classes, I am validating that each handle receives the correct cursor. I\'m taking the cursor calculation outside of `ElementNode.tsx` to align with the new state logic.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Cursor Refinement**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the crop handles. I have integrated the dynamic cursor classes, validated that each handle has the correct cursor, and I\'m updating the handle logic outside of `ElementNode.tsx`. I am now making sure that all the handlers work and display as expected.\n\n\n**Calculating Handle Position**\n\nI am now focusing on accurately positioning the rotation handle relative to the cropped image\'s center. I\'m leveraging `cropL`, `cropR`, `cropT`, and `cropB` to calculate the handle\'s `left` and `top` coordinates, offsetting from the image edges to ensure proper placement around the center of the cropped region.\n\n\n**Analyzing Handle Position**\n\nI am now analyzing crop handle positioning and interaction, paying close attention to pointer events. I\'ve confirmed accurate placement of the rotation handle. I\'m noting drag behavior outside the cropped area, wondering if that should be disabled. The current implementation implicitly allows dragging within the original image bounds, even outside the cropped portion. I am evaluating whether to limit dragging strictly to the cropped region, mirroring Google Slides.\n\n\n**Validating Click Behavior**\n\nI\'ve just validated the click behavior using `pointer-events-none` on the outer `div` and `pointer-events-auto` on the `img`. This configuration allows clicks on the visible image to trigger the `div`\'s `onPointerDown` while clicks outside the clipped area pass through to the workspace, perfectly mirroring standard presentation software behavior. This resolves the previous hit-testing issue and ensures that the cropped area acts as the active hit target.\n\n\n**Refining Click Interaction**\n\nI\'m now refining click interactions and `pointer-events` within the cropped image. My main observation is that if I set `pointerEvents: \'none\'` on the root `div`, in crop mode the outer, darkened image needs `pointerEvents: \'auto\'` to enable image dragging. I\'m noting the need to set `pointerEvents: \'auto\'` on the `img` with `clipPath`, the darkened `img`, the handles, and the rotation button. I\'m making a configuration that mirrors standard presentation software behavior.\n\n\n**Optimizing Interaction Logic**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the click interactions with the cropped images, specifically using pointer events. Currently, I\'m analyzing the proper interaction to ensure the darkened background image and crop handles work, especially after the `pointerEvents` adjustment. I must make sure that it mirrors the behavior of similar presentation software.\n\n\n**Validating Crop Interactions**\n\nI\'m now ensuring all aspects of crop mode work properly with pointer events. The challenge is making all clickable elements, including the image and crop handles, respond to interactions in the cropped region. It\'s working to have the outer element handle pointer downs while the image takes all the click events, to emulate the behavior of similar presentation software. This is critical for an intuitive user experience.\n\n\n**Refining Cropping Behavior**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `pointerEvents` on both the image and the surrounding div to ensure correct crop mode interactions. I\'m focusing on validating that the click behavior works by enabling the image to handle clicks while the container provides a hit target. This configuration mirrors behavior from Google Slides.\n\n\n**Refining Clipboard Implementation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the `COPY_ELEMENT` and `PASTE_ELEMENT` actions within the reducer, along with the initial state and types. I\'ve defined these in `src/types.ts` and `src/store.tsx` for proper handling of the `clipboardElement`. I\'m working to make sure these actions correctly manage the `clipboardElement` in the state.\n```\n\n**Finalizing Clipboard Integration**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the clipboard integration within my state management. I am reviewing the current implementation for `COPY_ELEMENT` and `PASTE_ELEMENT`. I\'ve made sure to correctly manage the new state within `src/types.ts` and `src/store.tsx`.\n\n\n**Implementing Key Bindings**\n\nI\'ve just finished hooking up the copy and paste key events in `Workspace.tsx` using `keydown` listeners, excluding input elements. I\'m now dispatching the `COPY_ELEMENT` and `PASTE_ELEMENT` actions in response to `Ctrl+C` and `Ctrl+V` respectively.\n\n\n**Refining Clipboard Implementation**\n\nI\'m now refining the clipboard implementation, ensuring copy/paste actions work with `clipboardElement`. I\'ve added `ElementData | null` to `AppState` to manage copied elements. I\'m focusing on validating state management and double checking the structure in `src/types.ts`. My key goal is that the application uses the local state and not the clipboard.\n\n\n**Finalizing Clipboard Implementation**\n\nI\'ve just ensured copy/paste integrates fully, using local state instead of the system clipboard. I\'ve validated the actions, state management, and the `clipboardElement` structure to complete this implementation.\n\n\n**Finalizing Pointer Events**\n\nI\'ve just validated crop mode interactions by enabling clicks on both the outer `div` and inner image, while ensuring the underlying element receives the `pointerdown` event, allowing correct dragging, clicking, and handle interaction. It works like similar software.\n\n\n**Validating Clipboard Behavior**\n\nI\'ve ensured that all copy/paste functionality and state updates are working. I am now validating the overall behavior of all related functionality within the application. I\'ve focused on ensuring the copy/paste actions work with local state. I am confirming a clean, efficient integration, confirming that there were no regressions.\n\n\n**Validating Clipboard Behavior*